//@version=6
// BTC ATTACK 93 - WILLIAMS A/D ACCUMULATION-LEADS-BREAKOUT LONG. A GENUINELY NEW MECHANISM, PER
// ATTACK 92's QUEUE ITEM 1 (remaining untried indicator families: ta.sar, ta.cci, ta.stoch, ta.macd,
// ta.cog, ta.tsi, ta.wpr, ta.iii, ta.wad, ta.wvad, ta.percentrank -- ta.linreg consumed at Attack 91/92).
//
// The stored scheduled prompt again describes a board state ("Attack 37, build its filter stack") more
// than 90 attacks stale, instructing "continue numbering after 37." The docs override it, again, per the
// prompt's own instruction: Attack 37 closed on cost at Attack 41; the OBV-divergence stack (66/68/82/83)
// is CLOSED at three terms; the SuperTrend family (89/90) and the linreg-channel family (91/92) are both
// discarded, the latter on an H1/H2 sign flip (PF 1.196 -> 0.835). Numbering continues after Attack 92,
// the last entry on the board.
//
// ==================== THE CLAIM UNDER TEST ====================
// ta.wad (Williams Accumulation/Distribution) adds or subtracts the full bar's TRUE RANGE each bar,
// signed by whether close moved up or down from the PRIOR close -- unlike ta.obv (which weights every bar
// by raw volume regardless of where price closed within its range) and unlike a pivot-based divergence
// (which needs two confirmed swings). The claim: when the WAD line prints a new N-bar high BEFORE price
// itself prints a new N-bar high, accumulation pressure (buyers absorbing range on up bars) is building
// ahead of the visible price structure. Entering only once price CONFIRMS that lead with its own N-bar
// breakout -- not on the WAD signal alone -- tests whether the lead has any predictive value, rather than
// just being a coincident restatement of the same price move. No prior BTC attack in this lab has used
// ta.wad, and this is a LEAD-CONFIRM latch, not a divergence (no pivot pairing) and not a bare price
// breakout (Attack 42/59, already tried) or an oscillator crossover (Attacks 57/58/64/79, already tried).
// Pine: `strategies/pine/attack93-wad-accumulation-breakout-long.pine`.
//
// ==================== MECHANICS, NO ARRAYS, NO USER-DEFINED FUNCTIONS ====================
// wad = ta.wad. wadBreak = wad > ta.highest(wad, lb)[1] (excludes current bar from its own reference).
// On wadBreak, latch `armed := true` and stamp `armedBar := bar_index` (HARD LESSON 8: setup and trigger
// on different bars, never the same one). While armed and within armBars bars, priceBreak = high >
// ta.highest(high, lb)[1] confirms the entry. INVALIDATION (HARD LESSON 8, generalised): if price prints a
// new lb-bar LOW while armed, the accumulation thesis is contradicted and the latch clears early, before
// armBars would have expired it anyway -- named explicitly so the "what else does arming trigger" question
// is answered, not assumed. Stop: the lb-bar structural swing low at the moment of entry (LESSON 5, never
// the WAD line, which this lab's fixed-SL/TP-at-entry rule forbids using as a trailing exit). Target: 2R,
// the lab's standing risk-reward setting (HARD LESSON 13: the R:R axis is neutral at best, do not retune
// it without a specific reason).
//
// AUDIT, one line per leg:
//   R >= 0.8% (LESSON 3) - EXCLUSION via rBig on rawR = close - swingLow, never clamped to a wider stop.
//   Stop beyond STRUCTURE (LESSON 5) - stopPx = ta.lowest(low, lb)[1] at entry, the swing low that stood
//        BEFORE the breakout bar, never the WAD value and never the entry bar's own low.
//   Each leg separately (LESSON 6) - LONG ONLY this cycle; short (WAD lb-bar-low lead + price lb-bar-low
//        confirm) is the standing structural asymmetry, queued for a future cycle exactly as Attack 91's
//        short was queued and Attack 64 named before it.
//   BINDING (E17) - three necessarily-binding terms: wadBreak (arms; removes bars where accumulation has
//        not led), priceBreak-within-armBars (triggers; removes armed windows price never confirms), rBig
//        (excludes; removes confirmed breakouts too close to their own stop). Each independently shrinks
//        the raw priceBreak count, which is why NO fourth term is added this cycle -- three unique domains
//        (volume-and-range flow, price structure, risk geometry) is the mechanism, not a filter stack.
//   REDUNDANCY (E14) - wadBreak and priceBreak read different inputs (ta.wad's cumulative signed true-range
//        vs. raw `high`), so neither is a restatement of the other by construction; that is the entire
//        thesis under test, not an assumption to wave past. rBig reads distance-to-stop, a third
//        independent domain from both.
//   LATCH IN SEQUENCE (LESSON 8) - `armed`/`armedBar` are var state set only on the WAD-break bar; the
//        price-confirm read happens on a STRICTLY LATER bar via the `armed` flag, and armBars=10 plus the
//        lb-bar-low invalidation both bound how long the latch can stay live. Never required on one bar.
//   OCCUPANCY (LESSONS 24/28/29) - pyramiding=1 (forced by engine), explicit `flat` check gates every
//        entry so a live position blocks a fresh arm-then-trigger sequence.
//   CASCADE (HARD LESSON 42/43) - single TP+SL per entry, no partials, no scale-outs; expect
//        totalTrades == unique entries, cascadeRatio 1.
//   SL and TP FIXED AT ENTRY. No trailing, no averaging down, no martingale.
//
// FREQUENCY ESTIMATE, REGISTERED BEFORE RUNNING (HARD LESSON 4): lb=20 (5-hour window on 15m) is the
// same lookback this board has used for prior swing/channel constructions (Attacks 37/59/91). Two
// independently-binding terms stacked on top of a base rate that Attack 91's single term alone put at
// 327/1591 bars (H1) suggests this fires LESS often than a bare breakout -- most likely INSIDE the
// ~60-350 workable band, plausibly toward its low end because the WAD-lead requirement is the harder of
// the two conditions to satisfy. To be confirmed against the actual count and reported against the band
// explicitly rather than assumed (three of the last five frequency estimates in this lab missed by 2x or
// more).
//
// OUTCOMES REGISTERED BEFORE THE RUN (LESSON 17). BARE MECHANISM, H1 ONLY (credits band 250-500 at
// get_credits, the never-tuned pre-2024 half, per the kill rule):
//   * H1 ABOVE 1.0 AND TRADES INSIDE ~60-350 -> the WAD-lead thesis has a real bare edge at a workable
//     frequency; queue an H2 run before any filter work (the mandate this board applied to Attack 37 and
//     Attack 91 alike -- both halves before a stack is earned).
//   * H1 ABOVE 1.0 BUT TRADES OUTSIDE ~60-350 -> report which direction and by how much; a follow-up
//     would widen/narrow lb or armBars toward the band, not add a filter yet.
//   * H1 BELOW 1.0 -> DISCARDED by the kill rule immediately. No H2 run, no filter rescue. ta.wad becomes
//     a consumed indicator family alongside ta.linreg (91/92) and ta.supertrend/ta.sar-shaped flips (89/90).
//   * A MAJORITY WIN RATE WITH ratioAvgWinLoss WELL BELOW 1.0 -> HARD LESSON 53's inverted-payoff shape;
//     report it as that, and compute the breakeven win rate (1/(1+payoff)) before calling it an edge.
//   REPORT GROSS, COMMISSION, ACHIEVED RATIO, AVG LOSER AND WIN RATE alongside net and max drawdown
//   (LESSONS 36/39 and the board's own three-drawdown-category taxonomy).

strategy("BTC Attack93 WAD Accumulation-Leads-Breakout Long", overlay=true,
     pyramiding=1, process_orders_on_close=true,
     commission_type=strategy.commission.percent, commission_value=0.05,
     initial_capital=10000,
     default_qty_type=strategy.percent_of_equity, default_qty_value=100,
     margin_long=100, margin_short=100)

lb        = input.int(20, "Lookback bars for WAD-high and price-high (bars)")
armBars   = input.int(10, "Bars the WAD-lead latch stays armed")
minRpct   = input.float(0.80, "Min stop distance as % of price (LESSON 3)")
rMult     = input.float(2.0, "Target as multiple of R (HARD LESSON 13: neutral axis, not retuned)")
maxBars   = input.int(192, "Max bars in trade")

wad = ta.wad

wadHighPrior   = ta.highest(wad, lb)[1]
priceHighPrior = ta.highest(high, lb)[1]
priceLowPrior  = ta.lowest(low, lb)[1]

wadBreak   = wad > wadHighPrior
priceBreak = high > priceHighPrior
priceBreakdown = low < priceLowPrior

var bool armed    = false
var int  armedBar = na

flat = strategy.position_size == 0

// Invalidate the latch first: a new lb-bar low while armed contradicts the accumulation thesis.
if armed and priceBreakdown
    armed    := false
    armedBar := na

// Expire the latch if the confirm window has elapsed.
if armed and not na(armedBar) and (bar_index - armedBar) > armBars
    armed    := false
    armedBar := na

// Arm on a fresh WAD-lead (LATCH IN SEQUENCE: set state here, read it on a later bar below).
if wadBreak and not armed and flat
    armed    := true
    armedBar := bar_index

longTrigger = armed and priceBreak and flat

stopPx = priceLowPrior
rawR   = close - stopPx
minR   = close * minRpct / 100
rBig   = rawR >= minR

goLong = longTrigger and rBig

var float slPx   = na
var float tpPx   = na
var int   barsIn = 0

if goLong
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    slPx     := stopPx
    tpPx     := close + rMult * rawR
    barsIn   := 0
    armed    := false
    armedBar := na

if strategy.position_size > 0
    strategy.exit("LX", from_entry="L", stop=slPx, limit=tpPx)
    barsIn := barsIn + 1

if barsIn >= maxBars and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all()
    barsIn := 0

plot(goLong ? 1 : 0, title="LongSignal")
plot(armed ? 1 : 0, title="ArmedLatch")
plot(wad, title="WAD")
